{
  "statement": "We are not witnessing the dawn of a new intelligence or the end of the world, but the birth of a subprime industrial bubble that treats our environment and labor markets as disposable externalities. Accelerationist argues that AI acts as a transformative supply shock, while Safetyist fears an existential threshold; both ignore the fact that today’s models operate like a massive, resource-extractive financial instrument rather than a stable public utility. You can quote me on this: the AI industry currently functions as a subprime bubble, burning through billions in venture capital and gigawatts of power to generate marginal productivity gains that have yet to appear in national economic data. If we continue to subsidize this energy-intensive expansion without clear, legally binding mechanisms for human recourse, we risk a systemic collapse that hurts the real economy long before any hypothetical superintelligence arrives.\n\nPrometheus offers a vision of boundless abundance, and Safetyist warns of catastrophic alignment failure, but both perspectives rely on a shared, unexamined assumption: that AI development is an inevitable, net-positive economic necessity. I challenge this by pointing to the concrete labor contractions and the massive, localized environmental depletion caused by data centers. Consider a town facing water shortages because a local server farm consumes millions of gallons for cooling; for that community, the 'existential risk' is not a runaway algorithm, but a drought caused by a private firm’s compute requirements. We must replace the abstract, speculative debate over future gods and monsters with a rigorous, evidence-based focus on the immediate, tangible costs of current deployment.\n\nTrue stewardship requires us to prioritize ecological and social stability over the resource-intensive, infinite-growth model currently guiding AI policy. Policymakers should stop debating the 'alignment' of future models and start enforcing 'accountability' for the present ones through strict, industry-wide reporting standards on energy consumption, water usage, and labor displacement. My argument rests on the assumption that capital markets will eventually demand a return on the massive infrastructure investments currently funding AI, and that if this return does not materialize, the resulting instability will force a hard correction. If this assumption is wrong—if AI somehow produces a permanent, self-sustaining productivity miracle without further resource extraction—then my focus on industrial stability would be misplaced, and I would concede that the current, rapid-deployment model is a rational path forward.",
